	"thenaloneinthesmokedulbmeethusmadehisgreatestworkatinyblueovalhecalleditmetocyhetookthefirstonehimselfwitnessestherewerenonesayhewasquietforaverylongtimeandthenhesaidaightheknewitwastoopowerfultoleavelyingaroundsohegaveittoawizardwhocouldbetrustedtoholditthewizardsnamewasmattias": "narrator/narrator_clone_then_alone_in_the_smoke_dulbmeethus_made_his_gre.ogg",  # Then, alone in the smoke, Dulbmeethus made his greatest work: a tiny blue oval. He called it metocy.  He took the first one himself. Witnesses (there were none) say he was quiet for a very long time, and then he said, "Aight."  He knew it was too powerful to leave lying around, so he gave it to a wizard who could be trusted to hold it. The wizard's name was Mattias.
	"": "narrator/narrator_clone_there_is_a_god_who_does_not_live_in_caelid_nor_i.ogg",  # There is a god who does not live in Caelid, nor in any heaven, but in a dimension folded behind the smoke. His name is Dulbmeethus. No temple can reach him and no prayer can find him. There is only one way: a traveler must light a Dulber's Frint Pack and breathe deep, until their spirit slips free of their body and drifts upward through the purple fog. Many who tried were never the same. Those who arrived found him lounging upon a throne of clouds, eyes like twin galaxies, already waiting. He leaned forward, and spoke the ancient greeting:
	"thereisoneroadnorththroughthelavapassandacrossitsleepsthediddychudgiantnobodyremembershimlyingdowntheoldestgoblinssayhewasalreadyasleepwhentheirgrandparentswereunemployedtheroadwasbuiltaroundhimthentheroadgaveupandjustwentuptohimandstopped": "narrator/narrator_clone_there_is_one_road_north_through_the_lava_pass_an.ogg",  # There is one road north through the lava pass, and across it sleeps the Diddy Chud Giant.  Nobody remembers him lying down. The oldest goblins say he was already asleep when their grandparents were unemployed. The road was built around him, then the road gave up and just went up to him and stopped.
	"theyshallseekthelittleblueovaltheyshallbeofferedalesseroneandoneofthemshallpocketitanywayagiantshallsleepacrosstheroadandtheroadshallwaitforhimtosaytwowordsatthetopofthemountainanoldwizardshallaskthemaquestiontheiranswershalldecidehowthefirstchapterendsthereisnowronganswerthereareseveralbadones": "narrator/narrator_clone_they_shall_seek_the_little_blue_oval_they_shall.ogg",  # They shall seek the little blue oval. They shall be offered a lesser one, and one of them shall pocket it anyway.  A giant shall sleep across the road, and the road shall wait for him to say two words.  At the top of the mountain, an old wizard shall ask them a question. Their answer shall decide how the first chapter ends. There is no wrong answer. There are several bad ones.
	"thisprophecywaswritteninthelibrarybytheonewhowritesonanightwhentheorbglowedandjohnsaidwriteithasbeencopied4000timeseverycopyisslightlyworsethisiscopy4001hearitreaderordontithappenseitherway": "narrator/narrator_clone_this_prophecy_was_written_in_the_library_by_the.ogg",  # This prophecy was written in the library by the one who writes, on a night when the orb glowed and John said "write."  It has been copied 4,000 times. Every copy is slightly worse. This is copy 4,001.  Hear it, reader. Or don't. It happens either way.
